{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>They shall judge.</b> Like יִשְׁפוּטוּ, with shureq in place of holem, like וְגַם לֹא תַעֲבוּרִי מִזֶּה (Ruth 2:8), like וְלֹא תַעֲבוּרִי. And R. Shlomo sought to distinguish between them but did not succeed. And we can say, grammatically: for we have seen חָדְלוּ פְּרָזוֹן בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל חָדֵלּוּ (Judges 5:7), with a dagesh in the lamed, because it is at an etnaḥ; נָזֹלּוּ הָרִים (Isaiah 63:19 [sic]), at the end of a verse; and he said תְּהָתֵלּוּ בוֹ (Job 13:9), because he reckoned it like the end of a verse, for the word בוֹ is joined with תְּהָתֵלוּ and there is only a single vowel. And likewise יִשְׁפְּטוּ הֵם, like יִשְׁפוּטוּ — because one may object that the force of etnaḥ is not like the force of the end of a verse: הַעַמִּים תַּחְתֶּיךָ יִפְּלוּ (Psalms 45:6 [sic]), ה\"י עַל יְמִינְךָ (Psalms 110:5), טוֹב עָשִׂיתָ עִם עַבְדְּךָ (Psalms 119:65), and many like these, and likewise it is not found at the end of a verse:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes Isaiah 63:19 as 'הרים נזלו' but the received text reads 'נזלו הרים'; word order translated as he cites it. Citations to Psalms carry the chapter:verse numbering as he gives them; the second and third Psalm references lack chapter numbers in the Hebrew and are resolved by context to Psalms 110 and 119 respectively."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:52.726995030Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}